1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,440
Thomas Edison called it his baby.

2
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:08,380
Its development ruined friendships, created empires,

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:11,540
and challenged the minds of America's most prolific

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:13,540
and competitive inventors.

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,380
Now, the invention that would bring opera

6
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,380
to the parlors of the working man,

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,020
the phonograph on modern marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,300
Mylan, Ohio, 1847.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:39,740
Here on February 11th, Thomas Alva Edison is born.

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,240
Al, as he's called, is a bright little boy.

11
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,780
He's a bright little boy.

12
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:00,100
Al, as he's called, is a bright little boy, a late life child

13
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,740
who will remain deeply attached to his kind

14
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:05,740
and understanding mother.

15
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,840
From his father, Edison inherits a restless mind

16
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,640
and an enduring zest for life.

17
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:15,760
As dramatized in an early silent film at age six,

18
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:19,360
Edison is full of questions, like how can a baby goose come

19
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:21,060
out of an egg?

20
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:26,100
His father explains, but answers only set the boy through

21
00:01:26,101 --> 00:01:30,040
thinking and experimenting.

22
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:32,480
This experiment is fairly harmless,

23
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:36,380
but ever since Al accidentally burned down the family barn,

24
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:39,840
his parents tried to keep a watchful eye.

25
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:43,120
Later in Port Huron, Michigan, where the family moves,

26
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:47,460
some experiments are more like pranks.

27
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,700
But when he's 11, young Edison is often in the family cellar

28
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:55,900
experimenting with chemicals instead of studying at school.

29
00:01:55,901 --> 00:01:59,000
Tom, as he's now known, had been to school.

30
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:03,600
But the teacher said he was inattentive, possibly stupid.

31
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:06,040
His mother pulled him out after only three months,

32
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:10,440
and Thomas Edison never went to school again.

33
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:13,260
When he is 12, Tom becomes a newsboy

34
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:17,180
on the Grand Trunk Railroad, which connects Port Huron

35
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:19,120
with nearby Detroit.

36
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:22,560
He's not content with selling other people's papers, however,

37
00:02:22,561 --> 00:02:28,200
so he sets up a press and writes and prints his own newspaper.

38
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:30,700
He also brings a few chemicals on board

39
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,740
and accidentally causes a fire.

40
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,640
According to legend, the conductor's beating

41
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,220
leaves the boy near death.

42
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,520
But more likely, Edison's hearing loss

43
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:45,780
results from an early case of scarlet fever,

44
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:48,320
or even from birth.

45
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:50,480
There would be no more experimenting

46
00:02:50,481 --> 00:02:52,020
on the grand trunk line.

47
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:55,220
But Edison would soon be back selling newspapers again.

48
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,020
One day during a stop at the St. Clemens station,

49
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:03,600
Tom notices the station master's little boy

50
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,800
and a runaway car heading his way.

51
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:13,200
As a reward, the boy's father offers

52
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:15,900
to teach Tom telegraphy.

53
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Unaware that he's been studying the subject

54
00:03:18,001 --> 00:03:22,180
and even built a few telegraphic instruments on his own.

55
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:27,240
Tom eagerly accepts the offer and begins hands-on training.

56
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:30,180
It will prove to be the first and possibly the most important

57
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:31,480
decision in his life.

58
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:39,680
The nation is in the midst of a great civil war

59
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:42,800
when 16-year-old Tom Edison lands his first job

60
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:45,460
as a Western Union telegrapher.

61
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:48,260
For four years, he wanders from one station to the next,

62
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:50,900
but he spends so much time reading about telegraphy

63
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,200
and dreaming up ways to improve it

64
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,740
that he often finds himself out of work with no clear plans

65
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,500
for the future.

66
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:02,640
So it was in 1868.

67
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,080
While other restless young men are heading west

68
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,420
to seek their fates and fortunes,

69
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:10,680
Edison decides to go in the opposite direction,

70
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:13,860
his destination, Boston.

71
00:04:13,861 --> 00:04:19,980
When Edison came to Boston, he was coming to one of the key innovation centers in America at that time.

72
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:26,640
Not only was it an important area in terms of telegraphy, second only to New York,

73
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:30,380
but more importantly, it was the center of a machine shop culture.

74
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:31,960
And this is what he found there.

75
00:04:31,961 --> 00:04:40,000
He found skilled machinists who were able to take inventions, turn them into working artifacts,

76
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,700
that then he could continue to play with until he got them right.

77
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,660
While supporting himself as a nighttime telegrapher,

78
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:50,000
Edison usually spends his days at Charles Williams' machine

79
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:53,400
shop, working on ideas for inventions.

80
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:56,900
Within a few months, Edison files his first patent

81
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,380
for an electric vote recorder.

82
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:02,920
It will be the forerunner of modern voting machines,

83
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:04,420
but not yet.

84
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:08,420
It was refused by Congress overwhelmingly.

85
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:11,080
This defeated what they were in Washington to do.

86
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:13,420
They wanted to filibuster.

87
00:05:13,421 --> 00:05:16,520
With an electronic vote counter, there was no reason for this.

88
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:19,420
Edison was horribly disappointed and vowed

89
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:22,500
that as a result of this fiasco, he would only

90
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:25,460
invent products for which there was a market.

91
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:28,760
If he couldn't sell it, he was not going to invent it.

92
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:31,600
Penniless, but optimistic as always,

93
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:33,740
Edison informs fellow telegraphers

94
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:36,840
that he is now a full-time inventor.

95
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,740
And he heads for New York, where he hooks up

96
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:42,040
with a noted electrical engineer, Frank Polk.

97
00:05:42,041 --> 00:05:45,480
Polk likes the budding inventor and provides him with a home,

98
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:48,380
introductions to investors and work,

99
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:51,480
manufacturing and inventing telegraphic devices.

100
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:57,180
The first invention that really pays off

101
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:00,700
is a stock printer that will revolutionize Wall Street.

102
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:04,000
To collect his pay, Edison goes to Western Union's lavish

103
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:08,600
New York headquarters, where he picks up a check for $30,000.

104
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:12,040
Within a month, the money is gone.

105
00:06:12,041 --> 00:06:15,200
most of it to equip a new factory and laboratory in Newark,

106
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,340
New Jersey.

107
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:18,080
It's a big operation.

108
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:21,980
And overseeing it all is 24-year-old Thomas Alva

109
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:23,280
Edison.

110
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:26,180
Before long, there's another big change.

111
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:32,000
On Christmas Day, 1871, Edison marries Mary Stillwell,

112
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:35,060
a pretty young woman known for her fetching figure

113
00:06:35,160 --> 00:06:37,640
and her friendly disposition.

114
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,700
After a brief honeymoon, Mary tries

115
00:06:39,701 --> 00:06:43,140
to become a proper 19th century housewife,

116
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:45,880
while her husband eagerly returns to his lab

117
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:49,640
and his first true love, working up inventions.

118
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,820
When he is not yet 30, Edison has a steady income

119
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:55,020
and a measure of fame.

120
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:57,020
But he feels restless.

121
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:01,460
And in 1876, he decides to move 12 miles south

122
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:05,460
to a quiet country place called Menlo Park.

123
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,160
Edison needed a place where he could both be separated

124
00:07:09,161 --> 00:07:12,060
but connected to the rest of the world.

125
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:15,400
And a place like Menlo Park filled the bill perfectly.

126
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,700
He was setting himself up not as a manufacturer,

127
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:21,600
but as a professional change maker,

128
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:24,600
making a living as an inventor.

129
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:27,320
In Menlo Park, Edison buys a large house

130
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:30,920
for his family, which now includes a daughter and son,

131
00:07:31,020 --> 00:07:34,920
Marion and Tom Jr. With a former telegrapher nicknames

132
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:36,920
Dot and Dash.

133
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:39,960
For the single men, there's a boarding house.

134
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:43,620
Just a few steps away is the office and library.

135
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:46,660
And just beyond that is the laboratory,

136
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:50,560
the heart of Edison's small communal village.

137
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:53,680
With generous financing, mostly from Western Union,

138
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:56,980
and with the best supplies and tools at hand,

139
00:07:57,080 --> 00:08:00,580
Edison and his team set to work on several projects

140
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:03,980
in the summer of 1876.

141
00:08:03,981 --> 00:08:06,880
One is a repeating telegraph, a device

142
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:10,820
to record an incoming message on a disk, then repeat it

143
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:13,620
to the next station down the line.

144
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:16,100
At the same time, Edison's also working

145
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:19,560
on a new transmitter for the telephone, recently invented

146
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:21,760
by Alexander Graham Bell.

147
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:24,540
Somewhat envious because Bell invented the telephone

148
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:26,740
before he could, Edison now wants

149
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:29,480
to solve Bell's main problem.

150
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:31,980
The voices can barely be heard.

151
00:08:31,981 --> 00:08:36,720
On the night of July 18, 1877, while Edison and his boys

152
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:38,780
are in the lab testing a diaphragm

153
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:41,320
from the telephone transmitter, Edison

154
00:08:41,420 --> 00:08:43,620
makes a momentous discovery.

155
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:45,920
He was playing in the laboratory one night.

156
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:47,820
And as they were playing with the diaphragm,

157
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:50,360
he put his finger under it and realized that there was enough

158
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:53,340
movement there that he might be able to cause an impression

159
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:56,940
in a piece of waxed paper or something else that would allow

160
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,940
him to, in a sense, repeat the message to play it once it was

161
00:09:00,941 --> 00:09:02,780
once it was recorded, to play it back

162
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:04,480
so it could continue over the line.

163
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:07,880
That night, Edison scribbles in his notebook,

164
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:10,580
there is no doubt that I shall be able to store up

165
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:14,120
and reproduce automatically at any future time

166
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:16,220
the human voice.

167
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:18,360
Full of confidence, Edison thinks

168
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:21,360
he will now invent a kind of telephone answering

169
00:09:21,460 --> 00:09:23,220
and repeating machine.

170
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:25,840
But he's actually about to invent something far more

171
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:30,800
original and revolutionary, the world's first phone

172
00:09:30,801 --> 00:09:37,800
By the time of his death, Thomas Edison held over 1,000 patents.

173
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:42,240
The phonograph will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

174
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:50,260
We now return to the phonograph on Modern Marvels.

175
00:09:52,260 --> 00:09:56,760
The path from idea to invention is rarely straight or smooth,

176
00:09:56,761 --> 00:10:03,760
or smooth, even at Thomas Edison's invention factory in 1877.

177
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:06,400
When Edison starts thinking about a phonograph,

178
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:11,140
he knows that he won't be the first to record sound.

179
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:14,140
A Frenchman named Leon Scott de Martinby had already

180
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,940
done that 20 years before on a machine he called

181
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:19,680
the phonautograph.

182
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:21,620
What Edison doesn't know, however,

183
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:24,380
is that another Frenchman, Charles Crowe,

184
00:10:24,381 --> 00:10:27,320
Crow had recently written a paper describing a way

185
00:10:27,321 --> 00:10:31,880
to play back sound as well as record it.

186
00:10:31,881 --> 00:10:34,940
But what Crow only conceives, Thomas Edison

187
00:10:34,941 --> 00:10:39,060
will achieve with a little help from Charles Batchelor,

188
00:10:39,061 --> 00:10:42,980
a British-born draftsman, mechanic, and problem solver,

189
00:10:42,981 --> 00:10:47,300
and John Kruse, a Swiss-born master machinist.

190
00:10:47,301 --> 00:10:49,680
Even with help, work on the phonograph

191
00:10:49,681 --> 00:10:52,320
begins very slowly.

192
00:10:52,321 --> 00:10:56,260
In fact, the word phonograph doesn't appear in Edison's notes

193
00:10:56,261 --> 00:10:59,080
until one month after he reached the conclusion

194
00:10:59,081 --> 00:11:02,120
that he could record and play back sound.

195
00:11:02,121 --> 00:11:04,080
In another two weeks, Edison reaches

196
00:11:04,081 --> 00:11:07,420
another important turning point.

197
00:11:07,421 --> 00:11:09,680
On September 7, he sits down and draws up

198
00:11:09,681 --> 00:11:12,360
what's essentially a list of possible things

199
00:11:12,361 --> 00:11:13,280
you can do with the phonograph.

200
00:11:13,281 --> 00:11:15,920
They're all things you would be familiar with today.

201
00:11:15,921 --> 00:11:18,500
But this marks the beginning of the phonograph

202
00:11:18,501 --> 00:11:21,500
as a completely separate technology.

203
00:11:21,501 --> 00:11:24,200
Two months later, Scientific American announces

204
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:27,740
that Mr. Edison has a wonderful new invention for a talking

205
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:29,780
machine.

206
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,520
It's a deliberate press leak written at Menlo Park

207
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:35,180
by a close member of the Edison team.

208
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:37,980
But so far, there is no phonograph,

209
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:42,320
not even a sketch of what it will be in its final form.

210
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:48,460
On December 3, Edison considers three different formats, tape,

211
00:11:48,461 --> 00:11:53,800
disc, and cylinder before making a final decision.

212
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:57,000
50 years later, he would recall.

213
00:11:57,100 --> 00:12:00,440
I designed my original tinfoil photograph

214
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:05,180
in cylinder form and gave it to my faithful John Kruse

215
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:06,440
to make.

216
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:08,580
He made fun of it.

217
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:11,220
Whether Kruse made fun of Edison's drawing or not,

218
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:14,060
he takes it and goes to work.

219
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:16,580
On December 6, he sends word to the boss

220
00:12:16,581 --> 00:12:19,580
that his new invention is finished and ready for a test.

221
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:26,220
In 1927, on the golden anniversary of the phonograph,

222
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:28,520
Edison remembers what he said.

223
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:33,540
The first words I spoke in the original phonograph,

224
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:36,540
a little piece of practical poetry.

225
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:39,640
Mary had a little lamb, its fleece was white as snow,

226
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:43,040
and everywhere that Mary went, the lamb was sure to go.

227
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:51,580
To everyone's surprise, Edison's words came back just as he'd

228
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:54,580
spoken them on the very first try.

229
00:12:58,620 --> 00:13:02,820
Edison later said he was never so taken aback in all his life.

230
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:06,840
But for now, he can't wait to spread the news.

231
00:13:06,940 --> 00:13:09,740
On the very next day, Edison takes the phonograph

232
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:12,540
to the New York Office of Scientific American

233
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,640
for the first public premiere.

234
00:13:14,641 --> 00:13:17,640
The event is a sensation.

235
00:13:17,641 --> 00:13:20,640
I mean, if you read the articles in Scientific American,

236
00:13:20,641 --> 00:13:23,640
for example, that announced the discovery of the phonograph,

237
00:13:23,641 --> 00:13:26,640
you're struck over and over and over again by the amazement.

238
00:13:26,641 --> 00:13:29,640
All of a sudden, you can record your voice

239
00:13:29,641 --> 00:13:32,640
and play it back at any time now, 50 years from now,

240
00:13:32,641 --> 00:13:34,640
a century from now.

241
00:13:34,641 --> 00:13:36,640
They were astounded by being able to do that,

242
00:13:36,641 --> 00:13:39,640
but they were also astounded by something else,

243
00:13:39,641 --> 00:13:42,640
that the apparatus was so basically simple.

244
00:13:42,641 --> 00:13:47,640
Edison's tinfoil phonograph is mechanically very simple,

245
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:51,620
and its operation is quite easy to understand.

246
00:13:51,720 --> 00:13:54,580
To make a recording, a person speaks into a horn

247
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:58,720
which directs sound waves towards a thin metal diaphragm.

248
00:13:58,820 --> 00:14:00,700
When the sound waves strike the diaphragm,

249
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:03,760
they cause it to vibrate.

250
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:07,600
And as the diaphragm vibrates, it also vibrates an attached

251
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:10,000
needle.

252
00:14:10,001 --> 00:14:13,240
As the cylinder rotates, the needle makes indentations

253
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:16,440
in a thin piece of tinfoil.

254
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:20,140
To play back the recording, the process is simply reversed,

255
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,240
in this case, using a separate diaphragm and needle.

256
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:26,320
By following the indentations, the needle and diaphragm

257
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:30,120
vibrate, sending the same sound waves back again.

258
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:32,760
Although the phonograph is very simple,

259
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:35,860
at first, many people think it must be magic.

260
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:40,000
It isn't long before Edison has a new title.

261
00:14:40,001 --> 00:14:42,540
the Wizard of Menlo Park.

262
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:45,400
He's an international celebrity, and everyone

263
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:49,380
wants to see and hear his magical machine.

264
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:52,120
In April, the Wizard takes a new demonstration model

265
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:55,140
to Washington to show members of Congress and the Patents

266
00:14:55,240 --> 00:14:56,220
Committee.

267
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:59,160
Later that night, he's invited to come to the White House.

268
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:02,580
President Hayes is so thrilled with Edison's invention

269
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:05,660
that he wakes up his wife so she, too, can record and listen

270
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,400
to her voice.

271
00:15:07,401 --> 00:15:10,440
It's 3.30 in the morning before the First Lady bids Edison

272
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:11,900
good night.

273
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,040
Before leaving Washington, Edison has his image

274
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:18,380
and his phonographs taken at the studios of noted Civil War

275
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:21,640
photographer Matthew Brady.

276
00:15:21,740 --> 00:15:23,740
A second photo is taken.

277
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:26,580
The man on the left is Uriah Painter,

278
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:29,860
one of a group of investors who buy the rights to exhibit

279
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:32,260
Edison's machine.

280
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:35,400
By the summer of 1878, however, public interest

281
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:39,940
in the phonograph is fading, and so is Edison's.

282
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:42,540
In October, he suddenly abandons all work

283
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:47,240
on the one invention that he will always call his baby.

284
00:15:47,340 --> 00:15:50,180
I think it's very interesting because it was more his baby

285
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:52,520
than his own kids who were his babies at the time.

286
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:54,980
He spent more time with his baby machine

287
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:58,420
than he did with his baby kids, all of them, three of them

288
00:15:58,520 --> 00:15:59,520
at the time.

289
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:02,420
And he nurtured that to a certain point,

290
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:04,760
like you would with a real baby.

291
00:16:04,761 --> 00:16:06,460
And then just like he did with his own kids,

292
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:09,260
he totally turned his back on it because he got bored.

293
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:11,660
He wanted to do something different and exercise

294
00:16:11,760 --> 00:16:16,000
his imagination more creatively.

295
00:16:16,100 --> 00:16:18,500
Edison's new passion is electricity.

296
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:20,780
And inventing the incandescent light bulb

297
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:24,180
and bringing light to people's homes and streets and industries

298
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:28,760
will be his consuming passion for almost 10 years.

299
00:16:28,860 --> 00:16:33,780
Then in 1881, after three years as a virtual orphan,

300
00:16:33,781 --> 00:16:38,720
Edison's baby is taken in by Alexander Graham Bell.

301
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:42,360
Well, Alexander Graham Bell, when he heard about the phonograph,

302
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:44,620
was astounded that he hadn't thought of it.

303
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:48,460
So after Edison had given it up, Gardner Hubbard,

304
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:50,900
who was one of the leading principals of the Edison

305
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:56,440
Speaking Phonograph Company and also Bell's father-in-law,

306
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:58,600
pushed him into working on this device and trying

307
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:00,840
to make it a practical instrument so that, in fact,

308
00:17:00,940 --> 00:17:02,500
they could exploit it.

309
00:17:02,501 --> 00:17:05,540
With prize money awarded by the French government,

310
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:08,600
Bell sets up a new lab in Washington.

311
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:11,880
He assigns the task for improving Edison's phonograph

312
00:17:11,980 --> 00:17:15,780
to his cousin Chester Bell, a chemist,

313
00:17:15,880 --> 00:17:19,580
and Charles Tainter, a scientist and instrument maker.

314
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:21,380
Working in secret, Tainter and Bell

315
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:23,620
produce the graphophone.

316
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:26,360
One key improvement is that the recording surface

317
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:29,700
is wax-covered cardboard instead of tinfoil, which could only

318
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:32,500
be played two or three times.

319
00:17:32,501 --> 00:17:36,740
The hand crank is also gone, replaced by a motor typically

320
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:38,740
driven by a foot treadle.

321
00:17:38,840 --> 00:17:40,780
Recognizing that the graphophone is really

322
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:43,340
a set of improvements on Edison's invention,

323
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:47,120
Bell suggests that they pool their patents and join forces.

324
00:17:47,220 --> 00:17:48,320
Edison is furious.

325
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:50,920
He says, under no circumstances will I do business

326
00:17:51,020 --> 00:17:53,380
with Alexander Graham Bell or his pirates.

327
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:56,020
Instead, he resolves to rescue his baby

328
00:17:56,120 --> 00:17:58,460
and make a new and better phonograph.

329
00:17:58,461 --> 00:18:02,160
and most important, a finely engineered machine

330
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:06,100
that will bear the name of the original inventor, the wizard,

331
00:18:06,200 --> 00:18:07,900
Thomas Alva Edison.

332
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:15,920
Edison's product name prevailed where his invention did not.

333
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:17,920
The name phonograph referred only

334
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:20,940
to his cylinder recording device, but Americans

335
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:24,420
quickly adopted it as the name for all record players.

336
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:26,820
The phonograph will continue in a moment

337
00:18:26,821 --> 00:18:28,320
on Modern Marvels.

338
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:36,260
We now return to the phonograph on Modern Marvels.

339
00:18:40,860 --> 00:18:45,300
In 1886, when Thomas Edison decides to reclaim the phonograph,

340
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:47,300
the one most associated with his name

341
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:49,540
was still in the future.

342
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:52,020
And when he starts thinking about his favorite invention,

343
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:54,180
he's too involved with other matters

344
00:18:54,280 --> 00:18:56,820
to give it personal attention.

345
00:18:56,821 --> 00:19:01,560
One matter is Edison's decision to leave Menlo Park.

346
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:04,720
He'd spent little time here over the past eight years

347
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:07,360
while working on electricity, and he's already

348
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:10,360
started building a much larger laboratory, office,

349
00:19:10,460 --> 00:19:14,800
and factory complex in West Orange, New Jersey.

350
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:17,280
Another matter is that Edison has recently

351
00:19:17,380 --> 00:19:22,420
taken a new wife, 20-year-old Mina Miller.

352
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:26,820
His reason for remarrying is not divorce.

353
00:19:26,821 --> 00:19:31,020
His first wife, Mary, had suddenly died from a brain tumor almost

354
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:35,020
two years before at age 29.

355
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:39,560
Unlike Mary, Mina comes from a distinguished and wealthy family.

356
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:41,660
So when the couple move into Glenmont,

357
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:44,380
their elegant new estate in West Orange,

358
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:49,040
Mina is as prepared as she can be to become the wife of a famous,

359
00:19:49,140 --> 00:19:52,020
if often neglectful, husband.

360
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:54,940
On his honeymoon, Edison scribbles a few ideas

361
00:19:54,941 --> 00:19:57,180
about improving the phonograph.

362
00:19:57,280 --> 00:19:59,140
But since he's still deeply involved

363
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:02,420
with electrical concerns, he hands responsibility

364
00:20:02,520 --> 00:20:06,680
for the phonograph to others, especially Ezra Gilliland,

365
00:20:06,780 --> 00:20:10,080
an old telegrapher buddy, fellow inventor, employee,

366
00:20:10,180 --> 00:20:14,560
and the closest friend Edison would ever have.

367
00:20:14,660 --> 00:20:16,500
They were known as Damon and Pythias.

368
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:19,200
That's how close they really were.

369
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:22,060
And they grew up together, and they were friends

370
00:20:22,061 --> 00:20:25,160
in young adulthood, and Gilliland helped introduce

371
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:29,160
Edison to his second wife, which is a very crucial thing.

372
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:32,200
In fact, Edison and Gilliland were so close,

373
00:20:32,300 --> 00:20:37,000
they built side-by-side winter homes in Fort Myers, Florida.

374
00:20:37,100 --> 00:20:40,120
Edison drew up the plans.

375
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:43,340
But to Gilliland's dismay after working on the phonograph

376
00:20:43,440 --> 00:20:46,920
off and on for more than a year, in late 1887,

377
00:20:47,020 --> 00:20:51,360
Edison suddenly decides that he's taking control.

378
00:20:51,361 --> 00:20:53,400
While his friend quietly seethes,

379
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:58,560
Edison throws himself full force into perfecting his baby.

380
00:20:58,660 --> 00:21:01,200
One reason for his decision is that the Bell Tainter

381
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:04,500
graphophone has recently been introduced as a dictating

382
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:10,120
machine and getting the kind of press that Edison loves.

383
00:21:10,220 --> 00:21:14,320
He knows the time to catch up is now or never.

384
00:21:14,420 --> 00:21:18,460
Catching up requires a massive effort and eight long months.

385
00:21:18,461 --> 00:21:24,160
But on June 16, 1888, after a legendary final 72 hours

386
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:27,700
of work, the perfected phonograph is finished.

387
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:33,700
At 5.30 that morning, according to legend,

388
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:35,680
he has this picture taken.

389
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:38,980
It's the most famous photo of Thomas Edison.

390
00:21:39,080 --> 00:21:41,480
But what a different image from the young inventor

391
00:21:41,580 --> 00:21:43,080
of 10 years before.

392
00:21:43,180 --> 00:21:45,820
Edison undoubtedly does feel weary.

393
00:21:45,821 --> 00:21:49,260
But the pose is calculated to present a new image,

394
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:52,620
Mr. Edison as the Napoleon of invention.

395
00:21:52,720 --> 00:21:55,260
He likes the image so well he hangs a color version

396
00:21:55,360 --> 00:21:56,660
in his office.

397
00:21:56,760 --> 00:21:59,760
And what has this Napoleon achieved?

398
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:03,880
The recording surface is now a wax cylinder instead of tinfoil,

399
00:22:03,980 --> 00:22:07,180
an idea stolen from Bell and Tainter.

400
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:09,980
But Edison has a far better compound,

401
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:13,740
and the wax can be shaved and reused hundreds of times.

402
00:22:13,741 --> 00:22:15,740
With a work of invention finished,

403
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:22,280
Edison sells rights to exploit the phonograph for $500,000.

404
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:24,880
He asks his trusted friend, Ezra Gilliland,

405
00:22:24,980 --> 00:22:26,980
to handle negotiations.

406
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:30,560
It's a fateful decision, because Gilliland negotiates

407
00:22:30,660 --> 00:22:33,820
a secret deal with the buyer, allowing him

408
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:39,060
to collect $250,000, half of what Edison will get.

409
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:41,240
When Edison learns of this secret agreement,

410
00:22:41,241 --> 00:22:43,980
he feels utterly betrayed.

411
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:46,880
The friendship is over.

412
00:22:46,980 --> 00:22:50,280
While deeply hurt, Edison is resilient and optimistic

413
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:51,780
about the future.

414
00:22:51,880 --> 00:22:55,880
He gears up to produce 200 phonographs a day.

415
00:22:55,980 --> 00:22:57,620
He thinks his invention will be mainly

416
00:22:57,720 --> 00:23:00,920
used as a dictating machine in offices.

417
00:23:01,020 --> 00:23:04,860
But his first big order is for something less practical,

418
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:08,240
tiny phonographs for talking dolls.

419
00:23:08,241 --> 00:23:10,740
With an imported bisque head and lifelike hair,

420
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:13,280
the doll is quite beautiful.

421
00:23:13,380 --> 00:23:15,940
However, there are a few problems.

422
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:21,680
The mechanism was really too fragile

423
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:24,380
to be used inside a child's toy.

424
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:28,000
The records were very soft, made of a brown wax material.

425
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:30,400
Any kind of bumping or jarring would break the record,

426
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:34,000
and the records were not replaceable by the doll owner.

427
00:23:34,100 --> 00:23:38,240
In addition, the sharp stylus that was used on the record

428
00:23:38,241 --> 00:23:42,940
would only allow one, two, three plays, perhaps, maximum,

429
00:23:43,040 --> 00:23:45,820
before the stylus actually cut through the record

430
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:48,320
and made the record unusable.

431
00:23:48,420 --> 00:23:51,920
Another problem is the quality of the recordings.

432
00:23:52,020 --> 00:23:54,420
Edison would later say that he found the voices

433
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:58,320
of the little monsters exceedingly unpleasant to hear.

434
00:23:58,321 --> 00:23:59,820
I'm so happy to hear.

435
00:24:07,160 --> 00:24:10,060
With little progress in solving these problems,

436
00:24:10,160 --> 00:24:15,440
Edison will give up and never make talking dolls again.

437
00:24:15,540 --> 00:24:16,800
No matter.

438
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:19,540
By 1890, the phonograph has already

439
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:22,720
found a home in concert halls where people

440
00:24:22,820 --> 00:24:24,720
gather to hear their favorite tunes.

441
00:24:28,320 --> 00:24:35,320
Sing-alongs were also popular.

442
00:24:35,321 --> 00:24:41,320
But the most popular and profitable use of the phonograph

443
00:24:41,321 --> 00:24:43,320
isn't in churches.

444
00:24:43,321 --> 00:24:47,320
It's in phonograph parlors and penny arcades,

445
00:24:47,321 --> 00:24:50,320
in hotel lobbies and train stations.

446
00:24:50,321 --> 00:24:52,320
By dropping a nickel in the house,

447
00:24:52,321 --> 00:24:55,320
the most popular and profitable use of the phonograph

448
00:24:55,321 --> 00:24:56,560
and train stations.

449
00:24:56,660 --> 00:24:59,560
By dropping a nickel into the slot and using the listening

450
00:24:59,660 --> 00:25:03,220
tubes, people can hear a rousing Sousa march, a Stephen

451
00:25:03,320 --> 00:25:06,160
Foster song, or a bit of 19th century humor.

452
00:25:22,220 --> 00:25:24,480
The popularity of coin and slot phonographs

453
00:25:24,481 --> 00:25:27,120
keeps Edison's factory humming.

454
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:29,980
Coin machines also boost the fortunes of the Columbia

455
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:34,020
Graphophone Company, for now Edison's only rival.

456
00:25:34,120 --> 00:25:36,620
And one message both companies repeatedly hear

457
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:38,020
is make more records.

458
00:25:40,860 --> 00:25:43,100
The phonograph creates thousands of new jobs

459
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:47,140
and gives birth to a new industry, music recording.

460
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:51,440
But the process is unbelievably primitive.

461
00:25:51,441 --> 00:25:54,440
And all the girlies he would take for a blow.

462
00:25:54,540 --> 00:25:57,040
With no way yet to mass produce records,

463
00:25:57,140 --> 00:26:00,040
at first every cylinder is an original.

464
00:26:00,140 --> 00:26:03,560
Performers must sing the same song or play the same tune

465
00:26:03,660 --> 00:26:05,460
over and over.

466
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:09,420
Sometimes it's possible to record on several machines.

467
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:12,860
But what kind of records are being made?

468
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:17,140
Mostly marches, minstrel songs, instrumental solos,

469
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:20,200
and sugar sweet songs like Edison's Favorite.

470
00:26:20,201 --> 00:26:22,600
I'll take you home again, Kathleen.

471
00:26:26,540 --> 00:26:28,940
What you really have here is somebody

472
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:32,220
with very, very traditional pedestrian,

473
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:34,820
or shall we say middle of the road tastes,

474
00:26:34,920 --> 00:26:38,060
that he believes this was what the rest of the American public

475
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:42,860
had an obligation to attend to coming from Thomas Edison

476
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:45,560
in his role as setting the tone, as it were,

477
00:26:45,660 --> 00:26:47,960
for what people should listen to in their homes.

478
00:26:48,060 --> 00:26:49,200
And if it was good enough for him,

479
00:26:49,201 --> 00:26:51,360
for him, it was good enough for everybody else.

480
00:26:51,460 --> 00:26:54,440
In fact, in 1893, most phonographs

481
00:26:54,540 --> 00:26:58,200
are still in recital halls and penny arcades.

482
00:26:58,300 --> 00:27:00,940
And very few are in private homes.

483
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:03,840
But the first home phonographs are not far away.

484
00:27:11,260 --> 00:27:14,360
In the late 1890s, phonograph parlors in Paris

485
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:18,460
featured request desks, where a customer spoke a request

486
00:27:18,461 --> 00:27:21,500
into a tube, and the disc jockey on the floor below

487
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:24,300
played the song into that particular customer's ear

488
00:27:24,400 --> 00:27:25,200
tube.

489
00:27:25,300 --> 00:27:28,800
The phonograph will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

490
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:36,320
We now return to the phonograph on Modern Marvels.

491
00:27:43,220 --> 00:27:47,020
In the mid-1890s, the latest novelty is motion pictures,

492
00:27:47,021 --> 00:27:50,020
and Thomas Edison is too busy developing motion picture

493
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,960
equipment and making movies to pay much attention

494
00:27:53,060 --> 00:27:54,660
to the phonograph.

495
00:27:54,760 --> 00:27:59,260
But revolutionary changes are underway.

496
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:03,140
One is that Columbia introduces the first graphophone

497
00:28:03,240 --> 00:28:05,280
designed for the home market.

498
00:28:05,380 --> 00:28:07,140
And what makes this machine possible

499
00:28:07,240 --> 00:28:09,840
is a new spring motor that results in a relatively

500
00:28:09,940 --> 00:28:14,780
lightweight and affordable machine, priced at $75.

501
00:28:14,781 --> 00:28:17,180
Shocked into action, it takes Edison two years

502
00:28:17,280 --> 00:28:19,780
to come up with his first home phonograph,

503
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:24,120
a superior machine priced at only $40.

504
00:28:24,220 --> 00:28:27,320
Two years later, there is the Edison standard phonograph

505
00:28:27,420 --> 00:28:29,420
at half the price.

506
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:33,760
Then the very next year, Edison brings out the gem.

507
00:28:33,860 --> 00:28:37,800
At only $10, it's the least expensive phonograph.

508
00:28:37,900 --> 00:28:40,600
But by the time Edison gets into the home market,

509
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:43,100
there is a completely new competitor,

510
00:28:43,101 --> 00:28:46,800
The Berliner Gramophone, the world's first disc record

511
00:28:46,900 --> 00:28:48,200
player.

512
00:28:48,300 --> 00:28:52,840
The creator is a young German immigrant, Emil Berliner.

513
00:28:52,940 --> 00:28:56,840
In 1877, Berliner invented a telephone transmitter, which

514
00:28:56,940 --> 00:28:59,680
is sold to Alexander Graham Bell.

515
00:28:59,780 --> 00:29:02,460
And 10 years later, he filed his first patents

516
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:06,160
on the disc record player.

517
00:29:06,260 --> 00:29:10,300
Like most patent models, the first machine was rather crude.

518
00:29:10,301 --> 00:29:13,200
But it already had one big advantage.

519
00:29:13,300 --> 00:29:17,300
It was relatively easy to mass produce flat disk records,

520
00:29:17,400 --> 00:29:21,420
and Berliner came up with a novel way to do it.

521
00:29:21,520 --> 00:29:24,020
There was a button manufacturing facility

522
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:26,420
called the Duranoid Plastics Company,

523
00:29:26,520 --> 00:29:29,520
and they stamped out buttons of various sizes.

524
00:29:32,380 --> 00:29:36,060
And my grandfather said, why not press disk records

525
00:29:36,160 --> 00:29:37,600
on the button machine?

526
00:29:37,601 --> 00:29:40,400
And so he did eventually.

527
00:29:40,500 --> 00:29:44,700
Two years after the invention, he began pressing records.

528
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:46,840
And they were five-inch disks.

529
00:29:46,940 --> 00:29:51,380
And interestingly, the compact disk that's so popular today

530
00:29:51,480 --> 00:29:54,920
is also a five-inch disk.

531
00:29:55,020 --> 00:29:57,980
By the late 1890s, Berliner has a better way

532
00:29:58,080 --> 00:29:59,520
of duplicating records.

533
00:29:59,620 --> 00:30:03,360
And his gramophone has a fine new spring motor.

534
00:30:03,460 --> 00:30:07,360
But in 1901, after surviving an exhausting patent suit,

535
00:30:07,361 --> 00:30:11,840
Berliner decides to sell all his assets to Eldridge Johnson,

536
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:14,340
the man who made his new motor.

537
00:30:14,440 --> 00:30:16,700
Along with Berliner's patents, Johnson

538
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:18,800
gets an incredible bonus.

539
00:30:18,900 --> 00:30:22,040
US and Canadian rights to a painting titled His Master's

540
00:30:22,140 --> 00:30:26,740
Voice, which Berliner had bought in England one year before.

541
00:30:26,840 --> 00:30:28,940
Ironically, the machine in the original painting

542
00:30:29,040 --> 00:30:30,520
was a phonograph.

543
00:30:30,620 --> 00:30:32,860
The artist had offered it to Edison's British company,

544
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:35,480
but they saw no sense or value in it.

545
00:30:35,481 --> 00:30:38,720
So for about $250, the artist simply

546
00:30:38,820 --> 00:30:42,120
painted a Berliner machine on top of Edison's.

547
00:30:42,220 --> 00:30:46,020
And now Eldridge Johnson owns a trademark that will soon

548
00:30:46,120 --> 00:30:49,920
become the most famous trademark in the world.

549
00:30:50,020 --> 00:30:52,000
As the 20th century opens, Edison

550
00:30:52,100 --> 00:30:54,300
is riding his own wave of success.

551
00:30:54,400 --> 00:30:57,040
He's now in control of selling as well as producing

552
00:30:57,140 --> 00:30:59,000
his phonographs and records.

553
00:30:59,100 --> 00:31:01,240
Sales are up to more than $1 million a year,

554
00:31:01,340 --> 00:31:04,540
and the first golden age of the phonograph is underway.

555
00:31:04,541 --> 00:31:07,180
Thanks to long years of research by his team,

556
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:09,940
Edison can now mass produce records.

557
00:31:10,040 --> 00:31:12,280
They're called gold cylinders because the process

558
00:31:12,380 --> 00:31:15,880
involves electroplating gold onto a wax master.

559
00:31:15,980 --> 00:31:17,780
In the coming years, Edison's company

560
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:22,200
continues to develop better and longer playing cylinders.

561
00:31:22,300 --> 00:31:26,300
From the very inception of Edison's business career,

562
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:28,800
he believed if he was going to produce a product,

563
00:31:28,900 --> 00:31:30,700
it had to be the best.

564
00:31:30,800 --> 00:31:33,340
From the very beginning, he was very proud of the name

565
00:31:33,341 --> 00:31:37,080
Edison, he put the name Edison on all of his products.

566
00:31:37,180 --> 00:31:39,280
And he felt if his name was on the product,

567
00:31:39,380 --> 00:31:41,840
the quality must be there.

568
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:44,680
While Edison is consumed with technical quality,

569
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:47,580
his competitors are more interested in satisfying

570
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:49,920
changing public tastes.

571
00:31:50,020 --> 00:31:51,920
Early in the 20th century, Victor

572
00:31:52,020 --> 00:31:55,860
begins adding operatic arias to its repertoire

573
00:31:55,960 --> 00:31:59,740
and signs an exclusive contract with a rising Italian tenor,

574
00:31:59,840 --> 00:32:01,100
Enrico Caruso.

575
00:32:03,340 --> 00:32:15,880
In 1906, the Victor Company makes another radical change.

576
00:32:15,881 --> 00:32:19,120
In the early 1900s, all of the phonographs

577
00:32:19,121 --> 00:32:20,620
had an external horn.

578
00:32:20,621 --> 00:32:25,560
It was a very large, very cumbersome, unwieldy affair.

579
00:32:25,561 --> 00:32:28,160
And housewives objected, number one,

580
00:32:28,161 --> 00:32:30,900
to the looks of these horns, number two,

581
00:32:30,901 --> 00:32:33,100
of the fact that they protruded into the room

582
00:32:33,101 --> 00:32:34,900
and people constantly bumped into them.

583
00:32:34,901 --> 00:32:38,800
And number three, they were horrible dust collectors.

584
00:32:38,801 --> 00:32:42,440
The Victor Talking Machine Company heard this complaint

585
00:32:42,441 --> 00:32:43,840
and they addressed it.

586
00:32:43,841 --> 00:32:47,660
They took the horn from outside the phonograph cabinet

587
00:32:47,661 --> 00:32:50,660
and placed it within the cabinet.

588
00:32:50,661 --> 00:32:54,700
So now it became part of a piece of furniture.

589
00:32:54,701 --> 00:32:57,860
We now had the first home entertainment system.

590
00:32:57,861 --> 00:33:01,460
the phonograph, the internal horn, and storage area

591
00:33:01,560 --> 00:33:03,400
for records.

592
00:33:03,500 --> 00:33:06,500
Although priced at $200, the Victrola

593
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:09,440
is an instant hit with housewives and their husbands,

594
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:12,080
and Edison has to respond.

595
00:33:12,180 --> 00:33:16,480
His answer is the Amberola, a very handsome machine, also

596
00:33:16,580 --> 00:33:18,040
$200.

597
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:20,520
But it plays cylinders, and by 1910,

598
00:33:20,620 --> 00:33:22,620
when the first Amberolas go on sale,

599
00:33:22,720 --> 00:33:26,980
cylinders are on their way out, even if Mr. Edison

600
00:33:26,981 --> 00:33:27,880
doesn't approve.

601
00:33:27,980 --> 00:33:31,120
Eventually, there's a greater and greater demand for discs.

602
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:33,780
Although he believes that the recording technology of cylinder

603
00:33:33,880 --> 00:33:35,820
is superior, discs have a lot of advantages.

604
00:33:35,920 --> 00:33:37,420
They're easier to use.

605
00:33:37,520 --> 00:33:38,920
They're easier to store.

606
00:33:39,020 --> 00:33:42,800
And so Edison, in fact, has to go and invent his own disc

607
00:33:42,900 --> 00:33:43,740
record.

608
00:33:43,840 --> 00:33:46,600
To do so, Edison will be spending less time at home

609
00:33:46,700 --> 00:33:49,740
with his wife, Mina, and more and more time at the lab,

610
00:33:49,840 --> 00:33:52,640
where his team has already begun work developing a disc

611
00:33:52,740 --> 00:33:54,480
machine and record.

612
00:33:54,481 --> 00:33:56,780
When Edison throws himself into the project,

613
00:33:56,880 --> 00:33:59,220
he often works around the clock.

614
00:33:59,320 --> 00:34:01,520
During the week of September 10, for example,

615
00:34:01,620 --> 00:34:07,120
he puts in 111 hours and 48 minutes, a pretty heavy load

616
00:34:07,220 --> 00:34:12,500
when you consider that Thomas Edison is now 65 years old.

617
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:14,900
As always, Edison's main interest

618
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:16,900
is in the quality of the sound.

619
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,540
But since he's deaf in one ear and can barely hear

620
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:22,040
out of the other, judging sound quality

621
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:23,940
poses quite a problem.

622
00:34:23,941 --> 00:34:27,140
Edison's solution is to sink his teeth into wood

623
00:34:27,240 --> 00:34:29,640
so that the sound can vibrate through his skull

624
00:34:29,740 --> 00:34:31,240
to his inner ear.

625
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:34,480
In fact, the marks on the framework of this disc phonograph

626
00:34:34,580 --> 00:34:36,880
test model were made by Edison's teeth.

627
00:34:39,680 --> 00:34:43,260
In the end, Edison and his team produced the finest disc record

628
00:34:43,360 --> 00:34:47,760
diamond needle reproducer and playback machine of its day.

629
00:34:47,860 --> 00:34:51,700
In 1917, Edison has good reason to feel perky.

630
00:34:51,701 --> 00:34:53,700
Sales have reached an all-time high,

631
00:34:53,800 --> 00:34:58,300
and the future looks rosy for Edison and his competitors.

632
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:01,540
But what they don't know is that the first golden age

633
00:35:01,640 --> 00:35:06,640
of the phonograph is about to end.

634
00:35:06,740 --> 00:35:09,280
In 1915, the number one record of the year

635
00:35:09,380 --> 00:35:13,560
was, I didn't raise my boy to be a soldier.

636
00:35:13,660 --> 00:35:15,560
Modern Marvels will return.

637
00:35:15,561 --> 00:35:22,560
We now return to Modern Marvels.

638
00:35:26,100 --> 00:35:29,200
As World War I comes to a close, Americans

639
00:35:29,201 --> 00:35:32,040
celebrate the chance to return to normalcy,

640
00:35:32,041 --> 00:35:35,320
in the words of the next president, Warren Harding.

641
00:35:35,321 --> 00:35:38,480
But with new fads, fashions, and prohibition,

642
00:35:38,481 --> 00:35:41,160
life in the roaring 20s will hardly be normal.

643
00:35:45,560 --> 00:35:48,900
Harding successor Calvin Coolidge declares that the business

644
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:50,860
of America is business.

645
00:35:50,960 --> 00:35:55,560
And Henry Ford agrees, rolling out a new Model T every 10

646
00:35:55,660 --> 00:35:57,000
seconds.

647
00:35:57,100 --> 00:36:01,500
The energy of the 20s is set to a new rhythm, jazz.

648
00:36:06,620 --> 00:36:09,120
Beginning unsegregated race records,

649
00:36:09,220 --> 00:36:11,660
African-American artists like Louis Armstrong

650
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:14,320
are creating a new American art form.

651
00:36:14,321 --> 00:36:16,060
art form.

652
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:19,020
Jazz records begin to cross the color line,

653
00:36:19,120 --> 00:36:24,560
and the phonograph begins to bridge black and white America.

654
00:36:24,660 --> 00:36:26,860
Americans are on a spending spree,

655
00:36:26,960 --> 00:36:30,060
and there's a tempting new product.

656
00:36:30,160 --> 00:36:33,280
Radio technology had been around for decades,

657
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:36,980
but in 1920, radio becomes a consumer product,

658
00:36:37,080 --> 00:36:40,980
and broadcast stations pop up all over the country.

659
00:36:41,080 --> 00:36:43,180
Thomas Edison's reaction?

660
00:36:43,181 --> 00:36:44,980
He hates radio.

661
00:36:45,080 --> 00:36:47,820
For Edison, the technology was the key,

662
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:49,720
the best quality sound.

663
00:36:49,820 --> 00:36:53,620
And that had always, that is why he resisted discs for so long.

664
00:36:53,720 --> 00:36:55,420
And it's also why he resisted radio.

665
00:36:55,520 --> 00:36:58,700
The sound quality was so bad that he thought, in fact,

666
00:36:58,800 --> 00:37:00,860
it would be harmful to the phonograph, especially

667
00:37:00,960 --> 00:37:02,500
since a lot of the early programming

668
00:37:02,600 --> 00:37:04,200
was playing recordings.

669
00:37:04,300 --> 00:37:05,840
So here was something that was, in fact,

670
00:37:05,940 --> 00:37:08,900
diminishing the quality that he had spent so many years

671
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:10,300
developing.

672
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:12,200
Despite tempting ads,

673
00:37:12,201 --> 00:37:14,640
phonograph sales plummet.

674
00:37:14,740 --> 00:37:19,980
While Edison declares radio is a fad, his son Charles disagrees.

675
00:37:20,080 --> 00:37:22,840
One of Edison's two sons with his second wife,

676
00:37:22,940 --> 00:37:25,940
Charles had been working for his father for several years.

677
00:37:26,040 --> 00:37:29,320
Now he's the acting head, and he argues that the company

678
00:37:29,420 --> 00:37:33,660
should start making radios like Victor and other competitors.

679
00:37:33,760 --> 00:37:37,260
His father responds with a resounding no.

680
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:40,000
Every single action that Charles Edison

681
00:37:40,001 --> 00:37:43,300
took in the purported role as head of this company.

682
00:37:43,400 --> 00:37:45,300
His father had to sign off on it.

683
00:37:45,400 --> 00:37:48,140
And he ended up writing a memoir called Out of the Shadow,

684
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:50,640
which really explains this relationship

685
00:37:50,740 --> 00:37:54,020
having to do with domination versus emancipation,

686
00:37:54,120 --> 00:37:56,480
a classic father-son struggle.

687
00:37:56,580 --> 00:37:58,260
The struggle continues and becomes

688
00:37:58,360 --> 00:38:02,260
more heated after 1925 when electronic recording is

689
00:38:02,360 --> 00:38:04,160
introduced.

690
00:38:04,260 --> 00:38:06,260
While Charles worries about the future,

691
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:09,100
Edison is out and about soaking up praise

692
00:38:09,101 --> 00:38:13,640
as the great inventor and the most famous man in America.

693
00:38:13,740 --> 00:38:18,340
He also enjoys well-publicized camping trips with his pals,

694
00:38:18,440 --> 00:38:21,340
Henry Ford and tire magnate Harvey Firestone.

695
00:38:26,280 --> 00:38:29,960
He doesn't seem to have a care in the world.

696
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:32,260
Whenever Charles can get his father's ear,

697
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:35,860
he continues asking if he can get into radio.

698
00:38:35,861 --> 00:38:40,600
In 1928, Edison finally gives in, saying, I'm telling you,

699
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:42,000
it's no good.

700
00:38:42,100 --> 00:38:46,600
But if you want to be a damn fool, go ahead.

701
00:38:46,700 --> 00:38:51,100
By 1928, radio sales have jumped to more than $500 million

702
00:38:51,200 --> 00:38:55,620
a year, and phonograph sales are still falling.

703
00:38:55,720 --> 00:38:59,220
When Charles brings out the first Edison radio in January

704
00:38:59,320 --> 00:39:03,260
1929, he has little hope that it will save the Edison

705
00:39:03,261 --> 00:39:04,500
Edison Phonograph Company.

706
00:39:09,100 --> 00:39:12,000
Nine months later, while Edison is in Dearborn, Michigan,

707
00:39:12,100 --> 00:39:14,680
with President Hoover for the 50th anniversary

708
00:39:14,780 --> 00:39:17,880
of the electric light bulb, Charles is in West Orange

709
00:39:17,980 --> 00:39:21,020
preparing layoff notices.

710
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:24,440
On October 29, he sends word to Edison Distributors

711
00:39:24,540 --> 00:39:27,820
and the trade announcing that the Edison Phonograph Company

712
00:39:27,920 --> 00:39:29,280
is no longer in business.

713
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:35,960
That very same day, the stock market crashes.

714
00:39:38,960 --> 00:39:42,100
News of the crash dominates the headlines.

715
00:39:42,200 --> 00:39:44,500
And not until nine days later do people

716
00:39:44,600 --> 00:39:48,100
learn that Thomas Edison will not be making phonographs

717
00:39:48,200 --> 00:39:51,920
or records again.

718
00:39:52,020 --> 00:39:55,420
By 1929, Edison had lived a full life.

719
00:39:55,520 --> 00:39:57,320
He knew it.

720
00:39:57,420 --> 00:39:59,020
He was by no means finished.

721
00:39:59,120 --> 00:40:01,320
There were things that he wanted to do.

722
00:40:01,321 --> 00:40:05,960
But he realized that he could not be the commander any longer.

723
00:40:06,060 --> 00:40:06,760
Happy?

724
00:40:06,860 --> 00:40:08,720
I'm sure he wasn't.

725
00:40:08,820 --> 00:40:11,860
But he was also realistic enough to understand

726
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:14,660
that this was the end of the road.

727
00:40:14,760 --> 00:40:17,740
There was nothing he could do to reverse the course that the

728
00:40:17,840 --> 00:40:19,580
Edison companies were taking.

729
00:40:19,680 --> 00:40:22,480
And it was a matter of turning his back and walking away

730
00:40:22,580 --> 00:40:23,680
from it.

731
00:40:23,780 --> 00:40:28,520
The 20s had been hard for Edison and his competitors.

732
00:40:28,620 --> 00:40:30,280
And in the early 30s, some people

733
00:40:30,281 --> 00:40:33,040
thought all phonographs would end up in the junkyard

734
00:40:33,140 --> 00:40:34,580
or in the attic.

735
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:37,120
But their predictions will soon be proven wrong.

736
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:45,260
In 1934, prohibition comes to an end.

737
00:40:45,360 --> 00:40:49,400
Alcohol begins to flow again.

738
00:40:49,500 --> 00:40:53,440
And the jukebox makes a grand appearance.

739
00:40:53,540 --> 00:40:55,100
Swing is in.

740
00:40:55,200 --> 00:40:58,680
And it seems like everyone feels like dancing.

741
00:40:58,681 --> 00:41:02,580
By 1940, there are 350,000 jukeboxes.

742
00:41:06,580 --> 00:41:11,120
Record sales soar as fast and furious as jitterbug dancers.

743
00:41:11,220 --> 00:41:14,900
Then in the late 40s, there's a major revolution

744
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:18,240
launched by one of Thomas Edison's oldest competitors.

745
00:41:21,440 --> 00:41:25,940
Come 1948, the Columbia Company

746
00:41:25,941 --> 00:41:29,680
introduced one of the greatest innovations in sound recording.

747
00:41:29,940 --> 00:41:30,680
There they are.

748
00:41:30,800 --> 00:41:33,700
They came right back in and said, we're not defeated.

749
00:41:34,300 --> 00:41:36,360
We've got an invention of our own.

750
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:39,380
And of course, it was the long playing record,

751
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:41,500
which was not made of shellac.

752
00:41:41,501 --> 00:41:45,280
It was made of plastic or vinyl or vinylite.

753
00:41:46,160 --> 00:41:49,220
And it was a marvelous, marvelous innovation.

754
00:41:50,280 --> 00:41:52,460
With the new long playing records,

755
00:41:52,600 --> 00:41:55,620
the old problem of running time seemed to have been conquered.

756
00:41:55,940 --> 00:41:59,040
Listeners could now enjoy more than 20 minutes of music

757
00:41:59,140 --> 00:42:01,540
on each side.

758
00:42:01,640 --> 00:42:05,480
But the LP, which runs at 33 and 1 3rd revolutions per minute,

759
00:42:05,580 --> 00:42:06,680
soon has competition.

760
00:42:09,680 --> 00:42:13,020
The 45 RPM single introduced by RCA,

761
00:42:13,120 --> 00:42:17,200
which had absorbed Victor during the troubled 20s.

762
00:42:17,300 --> 00:42:19,860
Each of the problems Edison had struggled with

763
00:42:19,960 --> 00:42:24,840
were being solved, culminating in the late 1950s

764
00:42:24,841 --> 00:42:31,840
with a new level of recorded realism, stereophonic sound.

765
00:42:31,940 --> 00:42:36,580
Then in 1982, after more than 100 years of continual incremental

766
00:42:36,680 --> 00:42:39,360
improvements on Edison's phonograph,

767
00:42:39,460 --> 00:42:42,700
a truly new recording technology appears,

768
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:44,500
the digital compact disc.

769
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:54,600
In 1878, one year after inventing the phonograph,

770
00:42:54,601 --> 00:42:57,640
Thomas Edison wrote down a list of 10 ways

771
00:42:57,740 --> 00:43:00,800
he thought it could be used to make people's lives richer,

772
00:43:00,900 --> 00:43:04,540
better, and more enjoyable, including talking books

773
00:43:04,640 --> 00:43:06,440
for the blind.

774
00:43:06,540 --> 00:43:08,240
It was a dream.

775
00:43:08,340 --> 00:43:13,160
But the man was so far sighted that in his own time,

776
00:43:13,260 --> 00:43:17,560
he couldn't accomplish what he really wanted to do.

777
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:22,160
But in later years, long after Edison's death,

778
00:43:22,161 --> 00:43:24,660
his dreams were fulfilled.

779
00:43:24,661 --> 00:43:27,000
The phonograph was Edison's favorite invention.

780
00:43:27,001 --> 00:43:30,960
And in many respects, that may be because it was

781
00:43:30,961 --> 00:43:34,300
the prototypical mythological invention.

782
00:43:34,301 --> 00:43:37,800
It was the one that sprang from nowhere.

783
00:43:37,801 --> 00:43:39,620
And that's what made his reputation.

784
00:43:39,621 --> 00:43:42,640
The phonograph is what made Thomas Edison Thomas Edison.

785
00:43:42,641 --> 00:43:45,980
And after the phonograph, now the greatest inventor of the age,

786
00:43:45,981 --> 00:43:47,860
as he was called as a result of that,

787
00:43:47,861 --> 00:43:50,920
could in fact do anything he wanted.

788
00:43:50,921 --> 00:43:54,620
Looking back at the years and inventions that followed,

789
00:43:54,720 --> 00:43:58,560
particularly the light bulb and motion pictures,

790
00:43:58,660 --> 00:44:01,360
it seemed there was no limit to what the great inventor

791
00:44:01,460 --> 00:44:03,260
could achieve.

792
00:44:03,360 --> 00:44:07,000
Even today, the legacy of Thomas Edison

793
00:44:07,100 --> 00:44:11,440
is as amazing as those sounds from the first tinfoil

794
00:44:11,540 --> 00:44:13,280
phonograph.

795
00:44:13,380 --> 00:44:14,680
Mary had a little lamb.

796
00:44:14,780 --> 00:44:16,420
Its fleece was quite as slow.

797
00:44:16,520 --> 00:44:20,920
And everywhere that Mary went, the lamb was sure to go.

